    "https://www.bard.edu/academics/faculty/": {
      "title": "Professor James Romm’s Translation of Greek Fragments Reviewed in theEpoch Times",
      "description": "Bard Faculty Overview and Search Form | Bard College Skip to main content. Bard Bard Menu Academics sub-menu Academics Programs and Divisions Structure of the Curriculum Courses Requirements Academic Calendar College Catalogue Faculty Bard Abroad Libraries Dual-Degree Programs Bard Conservatory of Music Other Study Opportunities Graduate Programs Early Colleges Admission sub-menu Admission Applying Financial Aid Tuition + Payment Campus Tours Meet Our Students + Alumni/ae For Families / Familias Join Our Mailing List Contact Us Campus Life sub-menu Campus Life Living on Campus: Housing + Dining Campus Services + Resources Campus Activities New Students Visiting + Transportation Athletics + Recreation Montgomery Place Campus Civic Engagement sub-menu Civic Engagement Bard CCE Engaged Learning Student Leadership Grow Your Network About CCE Our Partners Get Involved Newsroom sub-menu News + Events Newsroom Events Calendar Press Releases Office of Communications Commencement Weekend Alumni/ae Reunion Family and Alumni/ae Weekend Fisher Center + SummerScape Athletic Events About Bard sub-menu About About Bard: Administration Bard History Campus Tours Mission Statement Love of Learning Visiting Bard Employment Support Bard Global Higher Education Alliance for the 21st Century Bard Abroad The Bard Network Inclusive Excellence Sustainability Title IX and Nondiscrimination Inside Bard Dean of the College Giving Search Search Information For: Faculty + Staff Alumni/ae Families Students Giving to Bard Quick Links Apply to Bard Employment Travel to Bard Bard Campus Map Join the Conversation Ephraim Asili MFA ’11, associate professor of film and electronic arts; director, Film and Electronic Arts Program. Photo by Chris Kayden Bard Faculty Academics Menu Academics Menu Curric sub-menu Curriculum Structure of the First Year Courses Requirements Progs sub-menu Programs + Divisions Division of the Arts Division of Social Studies Division of Science, Mathematics, and Computing Division of Languages + Literature Interdivisional Programs + Concentrations Faculty Resources menu Resources Academic Calendar College Catalogue Libraries Dean of the College Additional menu Additional Study Opportunities Dual-Degree Programs Bard Abroad Graduate Programs Early Colleges Academics Home Bard’s extraordinary faculty are dedicated to the philosophy of teaching. Today and throughout Bard’s history, members of the faculty have effected change in medicine, the arts and letters, international affairs, journalism, scientific research, and education, among other endeavors. These distinguished scholars are advisers as well as instructors: Bard has no graduate teaching assistants. And the average class size of 16 in the Lower College and 12 in the Upper College allows for intimate discussions and one-on-one interaction. David Bloom ’13 MM ’15. Photo by Bruce Kung “What brought me to Bard, in a word, was the faculty.” “To work with Joan Tower, George Tsontakis, and James Bagwell was an opportunity I couldn’t miss. I had long followed and admired their work, and then I found out that each of them taught here. It’s easy for musicians to focus only on music, whereas I wanted to have a broader education that would prepare me for a world that requires a more well-rounded base of knowledge and experience.” —David Bloom ’13 MM ’15 Faculty News Bard’s New Chang Chavkin Center Director Roosevelt Montás Featured in the Chronicle of Higher Education “Montás may seem like he’s tilting at windmills. But a lot of other people and groups are galloping alongside him.\" Read More × Bard’s New Chang Chavkin Center Director Roosevelt Montás Featured in the Chronicle of Higher Education Bard’s new Chang Chavkin Center and Laura Y. Chang and Arnold Chavkin Professor in Liberal Education and Civic Life Roosevelt Montás were featured in the Chronicle of Higher Education . Photo by Inbal Sivan Bard’s new Chang Chavkin Center was featured in the Chronicle of Higher Education . The feature profiles Roosevelt Montás, the center’s director and Laura Y. Chang and Arnold Chavkin Professor in Liberal Education and Civic Life, and explores his philosophy of a broad, less departmentally siloed form of liberal education. As the former director of Columbia University’s Core Curriculum, Montás has an established track record of advocating for a model of undergraduate learning based on foundational texts and discussion-based courses. He teaches in the undergraduate college, leading research into the advancement of liberal education. His focus is on the importance of liberal education and the study of texts with major cultural significance related to fundamental human questions, emphasizing lives of purpose and deep civic engagement. “Montás may seem like he’s tilting at windmills,” writes Beth McMurtrie, senior writer for the Chronicle . “But a lot of other people and groups are galloping alongside him. They include foundations, longtime champions of Great Books curricula, and the many faculty members who have transformed their classrooms by adopting this approach.” Montás teaches in the Literature Program at Bard , which emphasizes cultural, linguistic, and geographic diversity, challenging national, cultural, and disciplinary boundaries. Read the story in the Chronicle Post Date: 09-03-2026 Bard Vice President Malia Du Mont ’95 and Professor Chris Gibson Discuss How Students Think About Civic Responsibility and Public Service on Radio Kingston “We want to help students have a sense of what the possibilities for the future are.” Read More × Bard Vice President Malia Du Mont ’95 and Professor Chris Gibson Discuss How Students Think About Civic Responsibility and Public Service on Radio Kingston Launching in the 2027 Spring semester, “A Republic, If You Can Keep It” will introduce new curricula, foster partnerships with other colleges and universities, and encourage public service. Photo by Kay Bell ’26 On Radio Kingston, Malia Du Mont '95, senior fellow at the Hannah Arendt Center (HAC) and Bard’s vice president for strategy and policy, was joined by former New York Congressman Chris Gibson, HAC senior fellow and a professor of political practice, to discuss the HAC’s new initiative, “A Republic, If You Can Keep It.” Appearing on Afternoons with Jami , they discussed the initiative’s mission to help students think deeply about citizenship, civic responsibility, public service, and productive engagement at a time of intense polarization. Launching in the 2027 Spring semester, “A Republic, If You Can Keep It” will introduce new curricula, foster partnerships with other colleges and universities, and encourage public service. At a particularly challenging moment in American history, the new initiative was described as reflecting the optimism inherent in teaching and learning, and the work of changing both oneself and society for the better. “We want to help students have a sense of what the possibilities for the future are,” said Du Mont. “We believe we can keep [the republic]. It’s hard work, we don’t think we’re about to lose it, we’re concerned, but we fundamentally believe and are dedicating ourselves to moving forward together.” The mission of the Hannah Arendt Center at Bard College is to create and nurture an institutional space for bold, risky, and provocative thinking about our political world in the spirit of Hannah Arendt. Its vision is to empower people to discover their unique opinions and political agency and also find common ground to build together a shared world through thinking, listening, and talking with one another. Listen on Radio Kingston Post Date: 09-03-2026 More News Bard Acting President Jonathan Becker Speaks About Student Voting Rights on the SpeechMatters Podcast × Bard Acting President Jonathan Becker Speaks About Student Voting Rights on the SpeechMatters Podcast Bard College Acting President Jonathan Becker argues that institutions of higher learning should explicitly support youth voting. Bard College’s Acting President Jonathan Becker appeared on the SpeechMatters podcast to discuss the ongoing struggle for youth voting rights in the United States. SpeechMatters is a project of the University of California’s National Center for Free Speech and Civic Engagement , and features guests who are tackling the issues of free speech and democracy on college campuses. Becker was joined by host Michelle Deutchman, as well as Yael Bromburg, adjunct professor of law at the American University Washington College of Law. They addressed a range of issues, including the history of youth voting and on-campus voting, the civic impacts of student voting, and the structural and psychological challenges to broader enfranchisement of young people. Becker argued that institutions of learning should not be neutral, but explicitly and forcefully in favor of student voting. “Young people are the future of our democracy, and would we rather have engaged citizens, or passive citizens who are not involved in our democracy?\" he said. \"I think if you believe in civic participation, then you believe in young people voting.” Becker, who coedited a recent book about youth voting rights , also spoke of his volunteer teaching experience in Kiev, Ukraine, following the fall of the Berlin Wall, and his dismay upon returning to New York's Hudson Valley to discover efforts by local officials to limit youth voting. “Having promoted democracy abroad, I discovered that our students here were systematically denied the right to vote locally,” he said. “As someone who spent half of their life abroad, the idea that we should be promoting democracy abroad, and coming home and some local official in Dutchess county is systematically denying students the right to vote is…outrageous.” Listen on SpeechMatters Post Date: 08-27-2026 Professor James Romm’s Translation of Greek Fragments Reviewed in the Epoch Times × Professor James Romm’s Translation of Greek Fragments Reviewed in the Epoch Times James H. Ottaway Jr. Professor of Classics James Romm and his book Since You're Mortal …: Life Lessons From the Lost Greek Plays. A new book by James H. Ottaway Jr. Professor of Classics and Director of the Classics Program James Romm, Since You're Mortal . . . : Life Lessons From the Lost Greek Plays , was reviewed in the Epoch Times . Romm’s book, which was reviewed earlier this year in the Wall Street Journal , translates fragments from ancient Greek literature and sorts them into themes about life lessons on love, politics, and more. These fragments come from the writings of Stobaeus, a fifth-century man who compiled lines from Classical writing to instruct his son; Romm’s translation is the first time they have been translated into English. “There is hardly anything more powerful or more beautiful than a great line,” writes Dustin Bass for the Epoch Times . “Romm’s new book gifts the reader these textual forms in all their beauty and wisdom.” Romm, whose previous books have been reviewed in the Washington Post and the Wall Street Journal , also recently reviewed Alexander: God, King, Man by Edmund Richardson for the Times Literary Supplement . He teaches in Bard’s Classical Studies Program , which seeks to understand the languages, literatures, histories, and visual and material cultures of the premodern Mediterranean world. The program approaches these ancient societies from a variety of disciplinary perspectives, including linguistics, art history, archaeology, anthropology, and philosophy, while also considering the long and complex legacies of ancient Greece and Rome in art, language, politics, and culture from antiquity to the present day. Read the Review Post Date: 08-26-2026 Bard College’s Leadership on Student Voting Rights Featured on MS NOW’s Deadline: White House and Way Too Early × Bard College’s Leadership on Student Voting Rights Featured on MS NOW’s Deadline: White House and Way Too Early A Bard student holding an \"I Voted\" sticker. Photo by Joseph Nartey ’26 MS NOW ran a segment on Bard’s history of student voting advocacy. MS NOW's chief political reporter Chris Jansing came to campus to speak with current students and Acting President Jonathan Becker for Deadline: White House . Becker discussed increasing restrictions on student voting including the proposed SAVE act, which would require voters to present a birth certificate or passport at the polls, and recent efforts to restrict mail-in ballots. “Restrictions on student voters aren’t simply happening, they’re growing. We’ve seen recent cases in Indiana, for example, where a law which bans college issued IDs from counting as identification for voting purposes will impact between 30,000 and 80,000 voters,” Becker said. “I’m grateful that I’m at a college where [voting] is more accessible, but it does worry me because if you’re at a place where it’s not accessible students are going to be more likely to not vote,” one Bard student told Jansing. Students, faculty, and staff at Bard have worked to secure voting rights on campus for over two decades, and secured a polling place on campus in 2022. Jansing also reported on Bard’s efforts to strengthen students rights on MS NOW’s morning show Way Too Early . Jansing mentioned Becker’s recent essay in the New York Times about student voting rights, “ What Does the Trump Administration Have Against the 26th Amendment ?” “The fight that they’re waging is just to be allowed to let students vote and use federal funds for that,” she said in reference to Becker’s piece. “[Bard has] a fantastic program that helps students learn how to vote, learn how to register, get involved. It took them 25 years and four lawsuits to get to where they are today.” Since 2014, the student-led group Election@Bard has educated students on their voting rights and organized to remove obstacles to voting. Election@Bard is an initiative of the Center for Civic Engagement at Bard College, which coordinates a broad range of initiatives that connect students to internships, volunteer opportunities, community engagement, and activism. Providing Bard students with the opportunity to tap into their own idealism and vision, and develop civic skills that are fundamental for active citizenship. Watch the MSN Deadline: White House Segment Watch the MSN Way Too Early Segment Post Date: 08-26-2026 Bard Rated Among Top Five Colleges Where “Professors Get High Marks” in Princeton Review College Rankings × Bard Rated Among Top Five Colleges Where “Professors Get High Marks” in Princeton Review College Rankings Bard's campus in the fall. Bard College has been ranked in the top five of the Princeton Review ’s 2026 list of colleges and universities for positive student sentiment about faculty. The “Professors Get High Marks” ranking is based on how strongly students at a school agree or disagree with the statement, \"Professors are interesting and bring their material to life.\" Members of the Bard faculty include Guggenheim, Pulitzer, and McArthur Genius Grant award winners. They have written critically acclaimed novels and plays, and effected change in medicine, the arts and letters, international affairs, journalism, scientific research, education, and more. Bard has no graduate teaching assistants, and the average class sizes of 16 in the Lower College and 12 in the Upper College allows for intimate discussions and one-on-one interaction between faculty and students. Read the Princeton Review Rankings Post Date: 08-26-2026 Professor Felicia Keesing Contributes to 35-Year Study Finding Lyme Disease is Spread More by Mice than Deer × Professor Felicia Keesing Contributes to 35-Year Study Finding Lyme Disease is Spread More by Mice than Deer Richard Ostfeld and Felicia Keesing, husband-and-wife researchers in upstate New York, are studying why Lyme disease and other tick-borne illnesses are getting worse. After more than 35 years, the Cary Institute for Ecosystem Studies has published the results of a long-term study into the ecology of Lyme Disease . The study, which was co-authored by Bard Professor and David & Rosalie Rose Distinguished Chair of the Sciences, Mathematics, and Computing Felicia Keesing and published in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, aimed to better understand how ticks, bacteria, climate, plants, and mammals interact to spread Lyme, a disease with nearly half a million new sufferers every year that affects the heart, joints, and nervous system. The study found that mice, not deer, may be the more relevant hosts of Lyme-carrying ticks, which could reshape how scientists think about tick-borne disease. “On balance, these surprises have enriched our knowledge of this system, helping us to inform management of tick-borne disease risk,” says Richard Ostfeld, the paper's lead author. The study, conducted on 2,000 acres of forest in New York's Dutchess County, is particularly noteworthy for having run continuously for nearly 40 years. Such long-term studies provide invaluable longitudinal data, revealing trends and patterns that shorter studies may miss. “Studies of this depth and length are incredibly rare,” says Shannon LaDeau, who co-directs the research program at the Cary Institute. “The evolution of understanding summarized in this paper is something you simply cannot get without such a long-term and system-focused study.” Read in Phys.org Post Date: 08-24-2026 Bard Acting President Jonathan Becker Writes Guest Essay for New York Times Opinion × Bard Acting President Jonathan Becker Writes Guest Essay for New York Times Opinion Students making signs at a polling location on campus. Photo by Jonathan Asiedu ’24. Jonathan Becker, acting president at Bard College, has published a New York Times Opinion guest essay about student voting rights. In the article, “What Does the Trump Administration Have Against the 26th Amendment?” Becker argues that institutions of higher learning have a duty to assist their students in navigating the registration and voting process, educating them not only about their democratic rights, but also actively helping them to realize those rights. He views this as especially crucial given new federal regulations set to take effect in October, in which the Office of Management and Budget has proposed to classify voter registration efforts by colleges and other nonprofit organizations as lobbying, which would curtail student democratic engagement. “American colleges and universities must not be neutral on the issues of democracy and civic participation,” Becker writes for the Times . “In the 2022 midterms, only 30.6% of college students voted: we should all view this as unacceptable. College leaders should articulate their opposition to the proposed rule changes and find resources to promote and defend the most foundational of democratic rights.” Read in NYTimes Post Date: 08-19-2026 Faculty Search Click the link below to browse through an alphabetical list of Bard Faculty Bard Faculty Search Name : Title : Biography : Interest : Division : Any Division of Languages and Literature Division of Science, Mathematics, and Computing Division of Social Studies Division of the Arts Interdivisional Programs and Multidisciplinary Studies Program : Any Africana Studies American and Indigenous Studies Anthropology Archaeology Field School Architecture Art History Asian Studies Bard Abroad Bard Center for Environmental Policy Bard Center for Environmental Policy (Affiliate) Bard Center for Environmental Policy (Visiting Lecturer) Bard College Berlin Bard Conservatory of Music Bard Conservatory of Music: Bassoon Bard Conservatory of Music: Cello Bard Conservatory of Music: Chamber Music Bard Conservatory of Music: Chinese Percussion Bard Conservatory of Music: Clarinet Bard Conservatory of Music: Composition Bard Conservatory of Music: Conductors Institute Bard Conservatory of Music: Dizi Bard Conservatory of Music: Double Bass Bard Conservatory of Music: Erhu Bard Conservatory o